# TODO

The work list with implementation details. The overview for people is in
README.md and doc/, the rules are in CONTRIBUTING.md and doc/developers/.

Priority says how important an item is for a usable window manager, not when
it will be done. The order inside a section is the order of work; `after:`
names a dependency. Every item needs a test (see CONTRIBUTING.md).

## Must

Window management that other programs can rely on.

### Repository and quality tooling (what is left)

- Try `tools/run-xephyr.sh` on another machine (never run without a display).
- Require the CI jobs in the branch protection of `master` (the CI is green,
  the protection is not set).

### ConfigureRequest and geometry

- From the code review: the first Tab of the run dialog reads the
  directories of `$PATH` in the handler (move it off the event loop, like the
  scan of the launcher); `tiny-shell --reload` trusts `_NET_WM_PID` of the check
  window (any client of the display can change it); a reload that changes the
  style and the XSETTINGS together publishes them twice; `sync_allows_resize`
  asks the server for the counter at every motion.

- `_NET_WM_SYNC_REQUEST` is used by the interactive resize by the edges
  (`Window::sync_request_resize`, `SyncGate`). Left: the size that waits for
  the client is applied at the next motion or at the end of the resize (an
  alarm of the SYNC extension or a timer would show it as soon as the client
  has drawn), the frame counter of the extended protocol (the second counter
  of `_NET_WM_SYNC_REQUEST_COUNTER`), and the resize by the keyboard, by
  `_NET_WM_MOVERESIZE` from the client and by a maximize.

- Work area: the start and the end of a partial strut are ignored (the whole
  edge is reserved); the screen size is read at each change (cache it,
  refresh by RandR); the minimum size from the hints is not honoured when the
  area is tiny.

## Should

### Panel and shell

- System tray, what is left: the balloon
  texts (`BEGIN_MESSAGE`), `_NET_SYSTEM_TRAY_VISUAL` for icons with an alpha
  channel, and a change of the clock while the shell runs must put the tray after it
  again (the clock is at the end only when it is set first).
- Drawing: `on_expose` creates and frees a `GC` each time. Keep one `GC` per
  widget (or per display) and draw into a `Pixmap`, then `XCopyArea`, to stop
  the flicker of hover in the panel and the dock. `Widget` gets
  `begin_paint()`/`end_paint()`.

- Dock mode `autohide`: a short delay before the dock appears and hides (now it
  reacts at once). The reveal strip is at the bottom edge of the primary
  monitor.

### Configuration

The ini file is there (see doc/configuration.md); these are the next steps. Unknown
keys are ignored by the reader, so keys can be added without a new format.

- More keys: `[commands] logout`, `[panel]` (layout; `clock_format` is there),
  `[plugins]` (the list of the plugins and their own sections, with the optional build of
  Lua). `[theme] style = dark|light` and `[theme] font` are there.
- Key bindings: the actions are in `ActionRegistry` (`lib/wm/action_registry.*`).
  Missing: more actions (`close-window`, `maximize`, `minimize`), and the key
  table of `Manager::set_events` / `on_key_press` / `on_key_release` (now hard
  coded Alt+Tab, Alt+Shift+Tab, Alt+;, Alt+F2, Super; `Manager::grab_shortcuts`
  grabs them again when the layout changes) becomes a table of bindings, a
  `[keys]` section (`Alt+Tab = next-window`) parsed with `XStringToKeysym`. Lua
  uses the same registry.
- Switching the colors (`Manager::set_variant`, the actions `theme-*`).
  Missing: a menu item or a key for `theme-toggle`, `tiny-settings` (the field
  for the style, and `tiny-fonts`/`tiny-settings` following it: their colors
  are fixed now), a switch by time of day.
- Reload without a restart (`reload_config` in `src/main.cc`). What is left:
  the scale and the font of the shell itself still need a restart (the sizes of
  the panel, the dock and the frames are computed once), and a key or a menu
  item for it.
- `tiny-settings` (`src/settings.cc`, the logic in `lib/app/settings_form.*`).
  Still missing: applying a setting at once when it can (the monitors and the
  wallpaper are applied at once, a running shell is told over X: a
  `ClientMessage` or a property on the root window; the dock mode and the
  clock are the first candidates), the other keys (`reboot`, `poweroff`) in the
  form and the theme (Appearance).

### Watching files

- Watching `ApplicationIndex` (and maybe the configuration) is a mix of the
  system mechanisms behind one small interface (`FileWatcher`: `fd()` for the
  poll loop and `dispatch()`): `inotify` on Linux, `kqueue` (`EVFILT_VNODE`)
  on the BSDs and macOS, event ports (`PORT_SOURCE_FILE`) on illumos/Solaris.
  A fallback with `stat` polling on a timer where nothing else exists.
  kqueue watches open files, not names: after an atomic `rename` the file has
  to be opened and registered again; for a directory compare `stat` after
  the event and rescan.
- A separate library for this is fine if it is widespread and probably there
  on every target system (it still needs a build switch, see CONTRIBUTING,
  Dependencies); we do not need an own implementation of everything.
  Candidates to check: `libinotify-kqueue`, libuv, libevent, efsw.

### freedesktop.org: desktop entries and launcher

- `ApplicationIndex` (`lib/xdg/application_index.{h,cc}`): rescan on
  `inotify` (`IN_CREATE|IN_DELETE|IN_MODIFY`) as one more fd in the poll loop
  (see "Watching files" above for the other systems);
  `OnlyShowIn`/`NotShowIn` (`tiny-shell` is not a desktop name; treat as
  unknown); `Path`, `Actions`.
- Launcher (the Applications item of the main menu opens it): group entries
  by the main categories (`AudioVideo`, `Development`, `Education`, `Game`, `Graphics`,
  `Network`, `Office`, `Settings`, `System`, `Utility`); do not implement
  `applications.menu` (XML merge rules), see the ideas at the end.
- Launcher: the recently used applications in a file
  (`~/.cache/tiny-shell/recent`, now only in memory), a button for the
  launcher in the panel, `Super` as a modifier of other shortcuts (now a
  shortcut with `Super` is lost).
- Firefox: the window cannot be moved by its title bar (other clients were not
  checked). Find out if it is the grab of the buttons in `Header`/`Window`
  and what Firefox does with the pointer.
- Search by `Exec` (`lib/xdg/application_search.*`) uses the program name of the first
  word, so `flatpak run org.x.App`, `env VAR=1 app` or `sh -c ...` match by
  the wrapper instead of the application. Skip the known wrappers (`env`
  with its assignments, `flatpak run`, `sh -c`) or take the class name.
  The search folds the texts at each call; fold them once in
  `ApplicationIndex::scan` if the measurement on old hardware shows it.
- Run dialog (`wm/run_dialog.cc`): Tab completes the names of programs from
  `$PATH` (`wm/command_completer.*`). What is left: file names after the first
  word and for a path (`./`, `~/`, `/`), and a list of the matches (now Tab
  walks through them). The dialog is centered on the primary monitor.

### Icons

- Icon theme: the theme from the configuration/GTK settings (`--theme` in
  `examples/applications` today), `.ico` given by absolute path (STM32 tools),
  `Context`/`Scale` of the directories.
- Symbolic icons that are installed but not used yet (`data/tiny-shell/icons`):
  icons in the items of the main menu (`system-shutdown`, `system-log-out`,
  `system-lock-screen`...), `window-shade` and `window-switch` for the shade
  button and Alt+Tab, the dialogs (`dialog-warning`...), `pan-*` and
  `system-search` in the launcher.
- Measure the icon loading on old hardware (Atom, Raspberry Pi 1, x86 32 bit),
  before anything is optimized. The estimate for a 512x512 icon scaled to
  48x48: `scale_image` ~0.7 ms (Atom), ~5 ms (RPi 1); the PNG decoding is
  10 to 40 ms and is the real cost, an SVG is more. Compare `scale_image` with
  `XPutImage` of the whole source + an XRender transform (it sends 1 MB
  instead of 9 KB, so it is probably slower without a GPU). Measure also the
  decoding (`stb_image`, `nanosvg`) and the whole launcher start. The real
  case is about 32 icons (8x4), bigger than 48 px. If `scale_image` shows up,
  use fixed point instead of `double` and `lround` (~5x on RPi 1); a box
  filter is better than bilinear for a big reduction (512 -> 48 aliases).
- Load the icons lazily and in the background: show the launcher at once with
  a placeholder, decode the icons that are visible first, maybe in worker
  threads (a part of the icons per thread; `IconTheme` is not thread safe, its
  caches would need a lock or one instance per thread); X calls stay in the
  main thread, the workers only give back an `Image`. Cache the finished
  pixmaps (for one size). To be designed after the measurement.
- ARGB icons: `_NET_WM_ICON` is converted to a `Pixmap` + mask. Keep an
  ARGB `Picture` and composite with `XRenderComposite` for smooth alpha
  instead of a 1-bit mask (`DockIcon::on_expose`, `TODO: move to wm/window`).
- Dock icons: `_GTK_APPLICATION_ID`, a changed
  `WM_CLASS` (it is read once, so the application of a window, its favorite
  place and its right click menu follow the first one), and the icon in the
  size of the dock only (`theme.wm_icon`).
- Dock: a tooltip with the name of the application, a keyboard way to the
  menu of an icon, and the dock follows a new or removed
  desktop file (with the `ApplicationIndex` rescan). The icons of the dock
  are made again at every change (cheap for a few icons); keep the ones that
  do not change if the flicker shows. The write of the file runs in the
  handler of the menu (a few hundred bytes); move it out of the loop if it
  ever blocks (a network home directory). A drag of a running application that
  is not a favorite into the favorites would add it.

### Font viewer

`tiny-fonts` (`src/fonts.cc`, the logic in `lib/app/font_browser.*`) is there; the
next steps:

- Show the styles of a family (bold, italic, the other widths) in the
  specimen, today only the regular one is shown (`FC_FAMILY` + the default
  weight). List them with `FcFontList` by the family.
- A keyboard way to the previous and next family in the specimen; search by
  more than the name (the style, the languages, the foundry).
- The info (the button Info) shows the texts of the main face only: the
  variable axes and the texts of the other faces are missing.
- Open the families in the background: `XftFontOpen` of the visible cells runs
  in the event loop (a few ms each); measure on old hardware, keep the cache
  across the scrolling (it is closed when it has more than 128 fonts).
- Sizes in points with `Xft.dpi` instead of pixels.
- Copy the name of a family (the selection, `XA_PRIMARY`).

### Quality

- Tests of the manager in `tests/wm/test_manager.cc` with fake clients
  (`tests/fake_client.h`) for every `_NET_WM_STATE` message.
- Measure and write down in doc/: resident memory after start, with and
  without windows (the binary size is checked by CI).
- Screen instead of `0`: `XDefaultDepth(dsp, 0)` and friends
  (`wm/window.cc`, `x_util.cc`); take the screen from the root window of the
  manager.
- Replace the `TODO` comments in the code that duplicate items here with a
  link to the item (`container.cc`: popover must stay on the root,
  `wm/dock.cc`: the click must end on the button, `wm/client.cc`: set state).

## Could

- Multiple monitors, what is left (the monitors, the panel and the dock on the
  primary one, the work area per monitor, maximize and fullscreen on the
  monitor of the window, the numbers on the screens and the layout by `xrandr`
  are done, see doc/configuration.md): a dock on every monitor (`[dock] monitors =
  primary|all`; it needs a `WindowList` model of the windows, then one `Dock` per
  monitor is a view of it), the panel on every monitor, a
  key for `identify-monitors` and a button for it in `tiny-settings`, a
  picture of the layout in the form (drag a monitor to the side of another one
  instead of the choice), the rotation and the resolution (now only `xrandr`
  by hand), a wallpaper per monitor, the start and the end of a partial strut
  (see the work area; a dock's strut now reaches every monitor along its edge), and the unresponsive dialog and the menus that are kept
  inside of the whole screen, not of one monitor.
- Virtual desktops: more than the one desktop that is published now
  (`_NET_NUMBER_OF_DESKTOPS`, `_NET_CURRENT_DESKTOP`, `_NET_WM_DESKTOP`,
  sticky windows work with it), switching, hiding the windows of the other
  desktops; pager in the panel.
- Lua scripting (optional build, `make LUA=1`, Lua 5.4): a `tiny` module with
  the action registry (`tiny.bind("Mod4+t", "exec:xterm")`,
  `tiny.action("name", fn)` = `ActionRegistry::add`), window events
  (`on_manage`, `on_focus`, `on_title`), read-only window information, and
  small widgets for the panel: a plugin makes a `Label`, `Button` or `Box`
  by name and puts it in the panel with `Panel::add_applet()` (there). The
  widgets need a way to be made and changed by name (a factory and setters for
  text, size and `on_click`), written when the first plugin is. No access to
  Xlib from Lua. The script runs in the main loop and must not block it (the
  timers are `Manager::get_timers()`). The library of the widgets
  (`libtiny-widgets.a`) is what the binding links with; it has no dependency
  on the shell. after: key bindings, configuration.
- Window snapping to the edges of the screen and to other windows while
  moving; half-screen maximize (Super+Left/Right).
- A simple animation of minimizing: the window disappears and its frame
  shrinks to the icon in the dock (or a snapshot of the window shrinks, if
  that is cheap). It must be possible to turn it off in `tiny-settings`. (The
  README, the user interface guidelines and "Not a compositor" say no
  animations: decide first, and change them with the feature.)
- Window menu, what is left: choosing an item with the keyboard (the arrows
  and Return; Alt+Space opens the menu, the pointer chooses), moving and
  resizing with the arrows (the keyboard directions of `_NET_WM_MOVERESIZE`:
  `SIZE_KEYBOARD` and `MOVE_KEYBOARD`), the menu of a minimized window from its icon in the
  dock.
- Session management / saving the layout.
- Focus follows mouse as a configuration option (click to focus is the
  default).
- Startup notification, what is left (the shell and other launchers start a
  sequence, the pointer is busy until the window comes or 15 seconds pass, see
  doc/usage.md): the dock marks the icon of an application that is starting
  (the sequence names the `WMCLASS`, `BIN` and `ICON`), the shell sends `new:`
  and `remove:` to the other watchers (pagers), a window that has the id only
  on its group leader or in `WM_CLIENT_LEADER` does not end the sequence, and
  the `_TIME` of the id could be the user time of a window that has none.
- A configurable cursor theme through `Xcursor`, only if it stays optional.
- Appearance for applications (light/dark, font, antialiasing, DPI): tiny-shell
  becomes the settings daemon and publishes one source of truth (the ini
  file) to every channel applications read. The design is below; the open
  questions are in "Ideas that are not decided".
  - XSETTINGS manager (`wm/xsettings.*`). Missing: the cursor
    (`Gtk/CursorThemeName`, `Gtk/CursorThemeSize`, with `Xcursor.*`),
    `Net/IconThemeName`, and the colors (`Gtk/ColorScheme` is only for GTK
    3.24+ with a new theme).
  - `RESOURCE_MANAGER` property on the root window (`lib/wm/xresources.*`).
    Read by an application at its start only; has no light/dark flag. Output
    only, the storage is the ini file. Missing: `Xft.dpi` and `Xcursor.*`
    (XSETTINGS has the DPI).
  - Portal `org.freedesktop.appearance` `color-scheme` (0 none, 1 dark,
    2 light; signal `SettingChanged`): the only real standard for dark/light,
    read by GTK4/libadwaita, Firefox, Chromium/Electron, newer Qt 6.
    Cheapest: write the gsettings key `org.gnome.desktop.interface
    color-scheme` and let `xdg-desktop-portal-gtk` relay it. Clean: our own
    `org.freedesktop.impl.portal.Settings` backend (a `.portal` file and
    `portals.conf`); more work, no dconf dependency.
  - Environment (`QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME`, `GTK_THEME`) only at the start of the
    session: it cannot change while running, so it cannot follow a switch.
  - Automatic switch by time: the shell rewrites the channels above; running
    applications repaint themselves.
  - Check what applications get now: `busctl --user call
    org.freedesktop.portal.Desktop /org/freedesktop/portal/desktop
    org.freedesktop.portal.Settings Read ss org.freedesktop.appearance
    color-scheme`, `xprop -root RESOURCE_MANAGER`,
    `xprop -root -notype _XSETTINGS_SETTINGS`.
- Default wallpaper: choose the 3840x2160 picture for a screen that is wider
  than 1920 px (`default_wallpaper()` takes the 1920x1080 one now), and offer
  the installed wallpapers in `tiny-settings` as a list.
- Wallpaper (the first version is `tiny-wallpaper`, see doc/configuration.md): what is
  left is to remove a wallpaper at once (now the plain color of the theme is
  back at the next login; `tiny-wallpaper` would need the theme color, or
  `tiny-shell` an action `wallpaper-clear`), a color around a centered
  picture (it is black), a picture per monitor (RandR, `XRRGetMonitors`), and
  a path field with completion in `tiny-settings` (a file browser is too much
  code for Xlib).
- Optional decoders, as `make` variables with the feature off by default,
  chosen by `pkg-config`: `libpng` instead of `stb_image` (about 21 kB less
  code in the programs, a faster inflate, but PNG only: it brings no new
  format), `libjpeg-turbo` for photos (much faster than `stb_image` on a 4K
  wallpaper), `libwebp`. Two code paths need a CI job of their own. For SVG,
  `resvg` (Rust, a C API in `crates/c-api`, a cargo build, megabytes, not in
  the distributions) instead of `nanosvg` only on request; `librsvg` is out
  (GLib, cairo, pango).

## Ideas that are not decided

Things to discuss before they go to a section above.

- Run the shell (panel, dock, launcher) as a separate process that uses only
  EWMH. It makes the window manager smaller and crash-proof, and tint2 and
  others could replace it, but the second process costs memory. Keep the
  boundary clean (CONTRIBUTING.md, Architecture) and decide when EWMH is
  complete.
- Themes: only two built-in, finely tuned themes (light and dark; they are
  there, `Style::colorize()` has the colors of both), no user themes and no
  CSS (decided to lean this way, not final; a third, custom one would be
  another `Variant` read from the ini file). Open: the light variant is a first
  version (the title bars and window buttons are light in both), and the real
  work is a matching look in GTK3, GTK4 and Qt, since each toolkit is themed
  differently; probably take a ready pair (Adwaita / Adwaita-dark for GTK,
  `QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME=gtk3` or Kvantum for Qt) and own only the colors of
  the shell. Also open: the storage format (ini; `.XDefaults` has no dark
  flag and does not change at run time), a switch by time of day, and the
  settings channels in "Appearance for applications" (Could).
- `applications.menu` (Desktop Menu Specification): leaning to "no", the
  category grouping is enough.
- Wayland / a compositor: out of scope (README). If any of these gets a
  reason to return, record it in `wont.md` with the condition.
